(5889422, 854960, 108584, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Crisik:</b></div><hr/><i> OK, so less effective than I thought but still much better than 4,4 </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I cant find the way to get to DPCers, Im stuck in a 4.6 average, but still trying! Thanks for your comments!</td>', 'Originally posted by Crisik: OK, so less effective than I thought but still much better than 4,4   I cant find the way to get to DPCers, Im stuck in a 4.6 average, but still trying! Thanks for your comments!', '2010-03-03 20:40:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(5889445, 855565, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Congrats on entering your first of what I know will be many entries here at DP Challenge. Not a bad shot for your first entry and certainly better than what I entered. <br/> <br/>Interesting scene laid out here and it definitely tells a story. The connection to the challenge isn''t immediately apparent from the photo itself so the title had to be used to reinforce what you are going for. Generally (in my opinion anyways), if you need to use your title to tell the viewer why it fits the challenge they probably aren''t going to be convinced anyways. <br/> <br/>Challenge aside the shot does have potential. The boy''s eyes take you straight across the frame to the spider on the left. The shed itself has interesting verticals. <br/>I like the panaramic crop as it does work with the scene as you''ve presented it. The use of the border is ok here in my opinion. Borders are used to help contain the image so it stands out from the grey (in the case of DPC) background. Its a good thickness too and doesn''t detract from the image. <br/> <br/>Unfortunately the biggest issue with this image is the background. You have a very bright object in the background and an object in a very distracting blue tarp. Tough to frame a shot and keep an eye on all those things but in some cases your background is even more important than your subject or at least just as important. Remember, just because your aren''t setting up all the elements doesn''t mean you don''t get to decide where they are when you look through the view finder. (i.e. move your feet!)<br/> <br/>Not too shabby for your first time. Keep shooting and we all look forward to your next submission!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Congrats on entering your first of what I know will be many entries here at DP Challenge. Not a bad shot for your first entry and certainly better than what I entered.  Interesting scene laid out here and it definitely tells a story. The connection to the challenge isn''t immediately apparent from the photo itself so the title had to be used to reinforce what you are going for. Generally (in my opinion anyways), if you need to use your title to tell the viewer why it fits the challenge they probably aren''t going to be convinced anyways.  Challenge aside the shot does have potential. The boy''s eyes take you straight across the frame to the spider on the left. The shed itself has interesting verticals. I like the panaramic crop as it does work with the scene as you''ve presented it. The use of the border is ok here in my opinion. Borders are used to help contain the image so it stands out from the grey (in the case of DPC) background. Its a good thickness too and doesn''t detract from the image.  Unfortunately the biggest issue with this image is the background. You have a very bright object in the background and an object in a very distracting blue tarp. Tough to frame a shot and keep an eye on all those things but in some cases your background is even more important than your subject or at least just as important. Remember, just because your aren''t setting up all the elements doesn''t mean you don''t get to decide where they are when you look through the view finder. (i.e. move your feet!) Not too shabby for your first time. Keep shooting and we all look forward to your next submission!', '2010-03-03 20:57:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(5889448, 856319, 63398, '<td valign="top">A LOVELY image!!! Congrats on your ribbon!</td>', 'A LOVELY image!!! Congrats on your ribbon!', '2010-03-03 20:58:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5889474, 856234, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club and congrats on entering your first Challenge!<br/> <br/>My first impression of this image unfortunately is the missed focus. My advice to remedy this? Well...shoot a lot. Shoot everything and anything. Fill up a card and just practise using your camera''s auto focus. Get a feel of how to get your camera to focus exactly where you want it. Open up the pictures when you get home and see how you did.  The side benefit of doing this is that if you get to shoot that once in a lifetime shot and you only have a fraction of a second to bring the viewfinder to your eye, frame the shot, get focus and fire you''ll be ready. <br/> <br/>The other thing to try is to use a bit more depth of field. Using a smaller aperture like f/8 would bring more of the scene into focus while leaving the background blurred out nicely. A large aperture (remember that smaller numbers for aperture mean a wider aperture) will get less depth of field which can be helpful but in cases like this less of your subject is in sharp focus. <br/> <br/>The composition is okay but it just doesn''t feel entirely planned. The top of the grass has been cut off and I don''t believe that''s intentional. I do like that you placed the grass on the one side and used the background fence to balance out the image. <br/> <br/>In terms of the challenge? I can see it but I don''t believe its the strongest subject for the challenge. Voters unfortunately like to be beaten over the head with the obvious. The less mental hurdles the better. (We voters aren''t that bright remember).  Still, it wasn''t totally unrelated and given the time of year its pretty tough to do better. <br/> <br/>Overall it was a good scene to shoot and definitely worth exploring. You did pretty good on your first challenge. good luck on your next one!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club and congrats on entering your first Challenge! My first impression of this image unfortunately is the missed focus. My advice to remedy this? Well...shoot a lot. Shoot everything and anything. Fill up a card and just practise using your camera''s auto focus. Get a feel of how to get your camera to focus exactly where you want it. Open up the pictures when you get home and see how you did.  The side benefit of doing this is that if you get to shoot that once in a lifetime shot and you only have a fraction of a second to bring the viewfinder to your eye, frame the shot, get focus and fire you''ll be ready.  The other thing to try is to use a bit more depth of field. Using a smaller aperture like f/8 would bring more of the scene into focus while leaving the background blurred out nicely. A large aperture (remember that smaller numbers for aperture mean a wider aperture) will get less depth of field which can be helpful but in cases like this less of your subject is in sharp focus.  The composition is okay but it just doesn''t feel entirely planned. The top of the grass has been cut off and I don''t believe that''s intentional. I do like that you placed the grass on the one side and used the background fence to balance out the image.  In terms of the challenge? I can see it but I don''t believe its the strongest subject for the challenge. Voters unfortunately like to be beaten over the head with the obvious. The less mental hurdles the better. (We voters aren''t that bright remember).  Still, it wasn''t totally unrelated and given the time of year its pretty tough to do better.  Overall it was a good scene to shoot and definitely worth exploring. You did pretty good on your first challenge. good luck on your next one!', '2010-03-03 21:35:38.000000', NULL, 0),
